  "worked_solution": "Let $H$ be the event that a randomly selected student attends a history course, and let $L$ be the event that they attend a language course. Then, we require $P(L|H),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(L|H)=\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(H|L)=0.45$ since $45%$ of language students also attend a history course.\n\n- $P(L)=0.55$, since $55%$ of students attend a language course.\n\n- $P(H)=0.65,$ since $65%$ of students attend a history course.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(L|H) & =\\frac{P(H|L)P(L)}{P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.45)(0.55)}{0.65} \\\\  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.",
